there are complex political issues that  are oversimplified to hide bad policy  and then there are simple economic  issues that are made more complicated in  order to hide bad policy tax cuts are  one that we have a really good grasp of  right assuming that you're in a mixed  capitalist economy when there's a pseudo  free-market basically if things are like  things are in the United States when you  give poor and middle-income people a tax  cut or a tax credit they mostly spend  the money because they need the money  they're sort of short on money so they  need the money and they're going to  spend that money if you go to rich  people and you give them a tax cut they  disproportionately save the money  because their needs are met right if  you're used to going to expensive  restaurants you're not likely to go to  many more of them because you get a tax  cut for example and if you give big  corporations a tax cut they will not use  the money to hire people all else being  equal companies hire workers when  there's demand for their product or  service after the Trump corporate tax  cuts we heard anecdotes about this  company is giving raises that one is  hiring more people but they were just  anecdotes and that's why we call it  merely anecdotal evidence or data we  heard a couple dozen stories about that  we now have broader data from the last  year from S&amp;P 500 companies and the S&amp;P  500 is it's an index it includes 500  large publicly traded companies it's a  broader index than the Dow it's a more  diversified index than the Nasdaq which  is very tech heavy and those 500  companies that are part of the S&amp;P 500  overwhelmingly spent their tax cuts on  buying back stock which increases the  value of that stock and helps the  executives and the rich people and they  spent that money on dividends which also  disproportionately helps their  executives and rich people and if you  know anything about basic finance and  economics you say of course they did  that of course they did that this is  such a simple thing if you think about a  tax rate as a cost ask yourself the  question would you rather avoid a high  cost or a local  a tax rate is the sort of opposite of an  interest rate that you make on money  that you have in the bank right if you  can choose between earning let's go with  Pat let's see if even Pat gets this and  I know now I don't mean that to be  dismissive I mean just let's see if Pat  understands what we're talking about  here if you can choose Pat between  earning two percent interest or four  percent interest on your money which  would you choose  I'll go with the four percent interest  why twice as much you make more money  Yeah right if you can choose Pat between  avoiding a two percent tax or avoiding a  four percent tax which would you choose  I'd like to avoid the four percent tax  and why is that it'll save me money  that's exactly right  when the tax rate goes down you are less  incentivized to avoid the tax by  investing in your company because those  investments are tax deductible when the  tax rate goes up you're more  incentivized to do things with the money  that will help you avoid the tax and  this came up during my interview with  Dave Rubin if you remember Pat like a  year or a year and a half ago it was a  moment of significant confusion that a  lot of people pointed out the only thing  here that's surprising is that the Trump  administration tried to say companies  would use the money for hiring when it  was obviously untrue and people bought  it because a basic understanding of  economics tells you if you lower the tax  rate on corporations it encourages  profit taking and it encourages just  buying up stock to drive the price up it  was obvious all along and shortly after  the Trump tax cuts were implemented they  did a story about how Morgan Stanley how  they put that extra money and I guess  the plurality of what they put that  extra money into or things like stock  buybacks and dividends yeah whereas only  like ten fifteen percent went to you  know raises and bonuses and the things  that we were promised and would like to  see happen well that ratio of like  roughly ninety ten or 85 fifteen that's  the ratio that the data more broadly is  pointing to which is that somewhere  between eighty and ninety percent of the  tax cuts to these big corporations have  been used to pay bigger dividends to  shareholders and executives and also to  buy back stock and last year we had  another report from the Institute for  Policy Studies which showed that when  you do a corporate  cut it boosts CEO and executive pay but  you don't typically see an increase in  jobs nor in the wages for the average  worker it's a really simple economic  concept that people who want corporate  tax cuts and tax cuts for the rich will  pretend is really complex in order to  get people on board with it  it's just bad policy plain and simple  and there is nothing surprising about  this except for the fact that people  keep falling for these obvious lies  discuss this with us on our subreddit  david pakman.com slash re d di t today's  program is sponsored in part by Blink  accom slash Pakman if you've not already  heard about plinkus it's amazing it's an  app for your phone tablet or web browser  and what they do is they take nonfiction  books popular nonfiction critically  acclaimed nonfiction they condense the  book into a fifteen minute audio book  you get all the most important  information and insights from each book  but you can soak up the entire essence  of the book in one sitting I've listened  to a ton of books on blinkin recent one  is a Robert Rice's new book the common  good also bunk by Kevin Young useful in  preparing me for the pseudo-science  miniseries that we're producing and if  you are a david pakman show viewer or  listener you can get a seven day free  trial by going to blink accom slash  Pakman if you're watching on youtube use  the link in the video desc